Next.js, Hono, Bun and Deno > Next.js (App Router)

Create `app/api/chat/route.ts`:

```typescript
import { chatcopsVercelHandler } from '@chatcops/server';

const handler = chatcopsVercelHandler({
  provider: {
    type: 'claude',
    apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY!,
  },
  systemPrompt: 'You are a helpful assistant.',
  allowedOrigins: ['*'],
  sessionSecrets: [process.env.CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET!],
});

export const POST = handler;
export const OPTIONS = handler;
```

The widget gets its session token from `/api/chat/session`, so add `app/api/chat/session/route.ts` with the same handler:

```typescript
// docs-check: skip, re-exports the route file above
export { POST, OPTIONS } from '../route';
```

The widget's `data-api-url` is then `/api/chat` on the same site, or the full URL from another origin.

Tools > Custom Tool Example > The flat parameters form

Tools written for earlier versions keep working. `parameters` is a flat map of string, number and boolean fields, converted to the same JSON Schema:

```typescript
import type { ChatTool } from '@chatcops/core';

const weatherTool: ChatTool = {
  name: 'get_weather',
  description: 'Get current weather for a city',
  parameters: {
    city: { type: 'string', description: 'City name' },
    unit: { type: 'string', description: 'Temperature unit', enum: ['celsius', 'fahrenheit'] },
  },
  required: ['city'],
  async execute(input) {
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.weather.example.com/${input.city}`);
    return { success: true, data: await response.json() };
  },
};
```

Storage > Retention > Express / Node.js

Use [`node-cron`](https://github.com/node-cron/node-cron) to call `purge()` on a schedule from the same process as your Express server:

```bash
npm install node-cron pg @types/pg
```

```typescript
import express from 'express';
import cron from 'node-cron';
import { Pool } from 'pg';
import { chatcopsMiddleware, createChatHandler, type ChatCopsServerConfig } from '@chatcops/server';
import { postgresStorage } from '@chatcops/store-postgres';

const db = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });
const storage = postgresStorage({ db });

const config: ChatCopsServerConfig = {
  provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY! },
  systemPrompt: 'You are a helpful assistant.',
  storage,
  retention: { conversationsDays: 30 },
};

const handler = createChatHandler(config);

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
app.use('/api/chat', chatcopsMiddleware(config));

// Run at 03:00 every day.
cron.schedule('0 3 * * *', async () => {
  try {
    const deleted = await handler.purge();
    if (typeof deleted === 'number') {
      console.log(`[chatcops] Purged ${deleted} old conversations`);
    } else {
      console.warn('[chatcops] purge() returned null: store does not support purge');
    }
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('[chatcops] Scheduled purge failed:', err);
  }
});
```

Alternatively, keep the purge logic in a standalone script and invoke it from an external scheduler (systemd timer, AWS EventBridge, GitHub Actions cron, etc.). Run database migrations separately as part of your deployment, not on every cleanup run:

Internationalization > Setting a Locale > Programmatically

```typescript
ChatCops.init({
  apiUrl: '/api/chat',
  locale: 'es',
  strings: {
    welcomeMessage: '¿En qué puedo ayudarte hoy?',
    greetingWithName: '¡Hola, {name}!',
    inputPlaceholder: 'Escribe un mensaje...',
    sendButton: 'Enviar',
    closeButton: 'Cerrar',
  },
});
```

Your `strings` go on top of the translation, and any string neither of them has stays in English.

Pre-chat Form > Submission callback and event

Use `onPreChatSubmit` in the widget config to receive the submitted answers in your application:

```typescript
const analytics = { track: (_event: string, _data: Record<string, string>) => {} };
ChatCops.init({
  apiUrl: 'https://your-api.com/chat',
  preChatForm: { enabled: true, fields: [{ name: 'email', type: 'email', label: 'Email' }] },
  onPreChatSubmit(data) {
    analytics.track('pre_chat_submitted', data);
  },
});
```

The widget also emits a `preChatSubmit` event with the same `Record<string, string>` payload. Subscribe with [`ChatCops.on()`](/widget/events#prechatsubmit):

```typescript
ChatCops.on('preChatSubmit', (data) => {
  console.log('Pre-chat form submitted:', data);
});
```

Pre-chat forms can only be configured through JavaScript. There are no `data-*` attributes for `preChatForm` or its callbacks.

AI Providers > Direct provider usage

To use a provider outside the server, create it from the same config and run a turn with it (see [Running a turn](#running-a-turn) below):

```typescript
import { createProvider } from '@chatcops/core';

const provider = await createProvider({
  type: 'claude',
  apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY!,
});
```

The provider classes aren't exported from `@chatcops/core`, so importing core doesn't load all three SDKs. Import them from their own paths:

```typescript
import { ClaudeProvider } from '@chatcops/core/providers/claude';
import { OpenAIProvider } from '@chatcops/core/providers/openai';
import { GeminiProvider } from '@chatcops/core/providers/gemini';

const provider = new ClaudeProvider({ type: 'claude', apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY! });
```

Storage > Retention

The handler exposes `purge()` for retention cleanup. `retention.conversationsDays` defaults to 30 days. Call `purge()` from a scheduled job or deploy task; it returns the number of deleted conversations, or `null` when the store can't purge:

```typescript
import { createChatHandler } from '@chatcops/server';
import { Pool } from 'pg';
import { postgresStorage } from '@chatcops/store-postgres';

const handler = createChatHandler({
  provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY! },
  systemPrompt: 'Be helpful.',
  storage: postgresStorage({ db: new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL }) }),
  retention: { conversationsDays: 30 },
});

const deleted = await handler.purge();
if (typeof deleted === 'number') {
  console.log(`Purged ${deleted} old conversations`);
}
```

`purge()` returns `null` only when a custom conversation store does not implement the optional `purge` capability, such as a custom store with only `get`, `save`, and `delete`. The built-in memory, SQLite, Postgres, and Redis stores all implement `purge()`.

Express Full Example > Complete Server

```typescript
import express from 'express';
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';
import { LeadCaptureTool, FAQKnowledgeSource, TextKnowledgeSource } from '@chatcops/core';

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

// Health check
app.get('/health', (req, res) => res.json({ status: 'ok' }));

// ChatCops endpoint
// app.use, so /chat/session (the widget's session token) reaches it too.
app.use('/chat', chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider: {
    type: 'claude',
    apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY!,
    model: 'claude-haiku-5-5',
  },

  systemPrompt: `You are a helpful customer support assistant for Acme Corp.
    Be friendly, concise, and professional.
    If someone asks about pricing, refer to our plans: Starter ($0), Pro ($29/mo), Enterprise (custom).
    If someone shares their contact information or wants to be contacted,
    use the capture_lead tool to save their details.
    Always be transparent about being an AI assistant.`,

  tools: [
    new LeadCaptureTool(async (lead) => {
      console.log('New lead captured:', lead.email);
      // Save to database
      // await db.leads.create(lead);

      // Send to webhook/CRM
      // await fetch('https://hooks.zapier.com/...', {
      //   method: 'POST',
      //   body: JSON.stringify(lead),
      // });
    }),
  ],

  knowledge: [
    new FAQKnowledgeSource([
      { question: 'What are your business hours?', answer: '9am-5pm EST, Monday to Friday.' },
      { question: 'Do you offer refunds?', answer: 'Full refunds within 30 days of purchase.' },
      { question: 'How do I contact support?', answer: 'Email support@acme.com or use this chat.' },
      { question: 'What payment methods do you accept?', answer: 'Visa, Mastercard, PayPal, wire transfer.' },
      { question: 'Do you have an API?', answer: 'Yes! Check docs.acme.com for our REST API.' },
    ]),
    new TextKnowledgeSource([
      `Acme Corp was founded in 2020. We build AI-powered customer support tools.
      Our flagship product helps businesses add intelligent chat to their websites.
      We serve over 500 businesses across 30 countries.
      Our team is based in San Francisco, CA with remote employees worldwide.`,
    ]),
  ],

  webhooks: [
    {
      url: process.env.WEBHOOK_URL || 'https://hooks.example.com/chatcops',
      events: ['lead:captured'],
      secret: process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET,
    },
  ],

  rateLimit: { maxRequests: 30, windowMs: 60_000 },
  analytics: true,
  allowedOrigins: process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS?.split(',') ?? ['*'],
  sessionSecrets: [process.env.CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET!],
}));

const PORT = process.env.PORT ?? 3001;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`ChatCops server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

Cloudflare Worker Full Example > Worker (`src/index.ts`)

To query a persistent vector store (such as Cloudflare Vectorize) alongside static knowledge sources, connect `workersAiEmbedder({ ai: env.AI })` to a vector retriever and add `workersAiReranker({ ai: env.AI })` to rerank its candidates with `@cf/baai/bge-reranker-base`:

```typescript
import { hybridRetriever, retrieverKnowledge, workersAiEmbedder, workersAiReranker, type VectorStore, type WorkersAiBinding } from '@chatcops/core';

declare const env: { AI: WorkersAiBinding };
declare const vectors: VectorStore;

const embedder = workersAiEmbedder({ ai: env.AI });
const retriever = hybridRetriever({
  store: vectors,
  embedder,
  reranker: workersAiReranker({ ai: env.AI }),
});
const knowledge = retrieverKnowledge(retriever, { topK: 5 });
```

See [Retrieval from a vector store](/core/knowledge-base/#retrieval-from-a-vector-store) for setup and retriever options. The [Workers AI reranker model](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/models/bge-reranker-base/) accepts the query and candidate contexts and returns their relevance scores.

Storage > Postgres > Neon

For Neon, use the adapter exported by the Postgres store with Neon's serverless SQL client:

```bash
npm install @chatcops/server @chatcops/store-postgres @neondatabase/serverless
```

```typescript
import { neon } from '@neondatabase/serverless';
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';
import { fromNeon, migrate, postgresStorage } from '@chatcops/store-postgres';

const db = fromNeon(neon(process.env.DATABASE_URL!));

await migrate(db);

const storage = postgresStorage({ db, tablePrefix: 'chatcops_' });

const handler = chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider: {
    type: 'claude',
    apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY!,
  },
  systemPrompt: 'You are a helpful customer support assistant.',
  storage,
});
```

Knowledge Base > Retrieval from a vector store

Embedders turn text into vectors. `openaiEmbedder` calls OpenAI's embeddings API (`text-embedding-3-small` at 1024 dimensions by default), while `workersAiEmbedder` runs inside Cloudflare Workers using the native `env.AI` binding (`@cf/baai/bge-m3` at 1024 dimensions by default, batched up to 100 texts). A retriever searches a vector store with them, and `retrieverKnowledge` turns the retriever into a knowledge source:

```typescript
import { hybridRetriever, memoryVectors, openaiEmbedder, retrieverKnowledge } from '@chatcops/core';
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';

const embedder = openaiEmbedder({ apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY! });
const vectors = memoryVectors({ dimensions: embedder.dimensions });

const handler = chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY! },
  systemPrompt: 'Answer questions about Acme.',
  knowledge: [retrieverKnowledge(hybridRetriever({ store: vectors, embedder }), { topK: 5 })],
});
```

`geminiEmbedder` does the same with [Google's Gemini API](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/embeddings). It defaults to `gemini-embedding-001` at 1024 dimensions:

```typescript
import { geminiEmbedder } from '@chatcops/core';

const embedder = geminiEmbedder({ apiKey: process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY! });
```

`voyageEmbedder` does the same with [Voyage AI](https://docs.voyageai.com/reference/embeddings-api). It uses `voyage-4-large` at 1024 dimensions by default, and tells Voyage whether it is embedding a document or a search query, which Voyage embeds differently:

```typescript
import { voyageEmbedder } from '@chatcops/core';

const embedder = voyageEmbedder({ apiKey: process.env.VOYAGE_API_KEY! });
```

Events > Use Cases > Conditional Display

```typescript
// Only show chat on certain pages
if (window.location.pathname.startsWith('/support')) {
  ChatCops.init({ apiUrl: '/api/chat' });
}
```

AI Providers > OpenAI-compatible servers

Any server that speaks the OpenAI chat completions API works through the `openai-compatible` provider. `baseURL` and `model` are required, `apiKey` only if the server wants one:

```typescript
import type { ProviderConfig } from '@chatcops/core';

const providers = {
  // Ollama on your own machine
  ollama: {
    type: 'openai-compatible',
    model: 'llama3.3',
    baseURL: 'http://localhost:11434/v1',
  },

  // OpenRouter, with the headers it uses to identify your app
  openrouter: {
    type: 'openai-compatible',
    apiKey: process.env.OPENROUTER_API_KEY!,
    model: 'anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5',
    baseURL: 'https://openrouter.ai/api/v1',
    headers: { 'HTTP-Referer': 'https://example.com', 'X-Title': 'My site' },
  },

  // A model that can't call tools
  withoutTools: {
    type: 'openai-compatible',
    model: 'gemma3',
    baseURL: 'http://localhost:11434/v1',
    supportsTools: false,
  },
} satisfies Record<string, ProviderConfig>;
```

`openai-compatible` sends `max_tokens`. If the server wants `max_completion_tokens`, set `maxTokensParam: 'max_completion_tokens'`.

Theming > Custom colors

`bgColor` and `textColor` replace the background and text in every mode, and the theme engine derives the other surfaces from them:

- **Surface:** lightened by 8% from `bgColor`
- **Input:** lightened by 14% from `bgColor`
- **Accent hover:** darkened by 10% from `accent` (and from `accentDark`)

```typescript
ChatCops.init({
  apiUrl: '/api/chat',
  theme: {
    accent: '#e11d48',
    textColor: '#FAFAF9',
    bgColor: '#18181b',
  },
});
```

Upgrading from 0.5 > Things to check > Provider classes moved to subpaths

Importing `@chatcops/core` no longer loads the Claude, OpenAI and Gemini SDKs, which cuts serverless cold starts. If you import the classes directly, change the import:

```typescript
// 0.4 used the core entry point; this import no longer exists in current releases.
// import { ClaudeProvider } from '@chatcops/core';

// 0.5
import { ClaudeProvider } from '@chatcops/core/providers/claude';
```

The same goes for `@chatcops/core/providers/openai` and `@chatcops/core/providers/gemini`. `createProvider()` and the server's `provider: { type: ... }` config don't change.

Tools > How Tools Work

1. The AI decides when to call a tool based on conversation context
2. ChatCops converts tool definitions to the provider's native format
3. When the AI invokes a tool, `execute()` runs server-side
4. The result is fed back to the AI, which formulates a response
5. Visitors only see the final message. The tool calls and their results are saved with it, so the next reply knows what the tools returned.

All the calls the model makes in one step run at the same time. A tool that takes longer than `toolTimeoutMs` (default 30 seconds) gets an error result, and its `signal` aborts.

When a tool throws, the model only hears `Tool failed: <name>`. What a tool throws can hold anything, such as a database error with a connection string, and the model could repeat it to the visitor. The full error goes to the server's [logger](/server/setup/#logging) as a `tool_error`. To tell the model something it can pass on, return a failure instead of throwing:

```typescript
import type { ToolResult } from '@chatcops/core';

declare function findOrder(id: string): Promise<{ status: string } | undefined>;

async function lookupOrder(id: string): Promise<ToolResult> {
  const order = await findOrder(id);
  if (!order) return { success: false, message: `There's no order ${id}. Ask the visitor to check the number.` };
  return { success: true, data: order };
}
```

Tools get the server's logger as `context.logger` too, for problems they handle themselves.

Cloudflare Worker Full Example > Worker (`src/index.ts`)

```typescript
import { chatcopsCloudflareHandler } from '@chatcops/server';
import {
  FAQKnowledgeSource,
  LeadCaptureTool,
  TextKnowledgeSource,
  type WorkersAiBinding,
} from '@chatcops/core';
import { cloudflareStorage, ChatCopsCounter } from '@chatcops/store-cloudflare';

// Cloudflare finds the Durable Object class through the Worker's exports.
export { ChatCopsCounter };

interface Env {
  DB: D1Database;
  COUNTERS: DurableObjectNamespace<ChatCopsCounter>;
  AI: WorkersAiBinding;
  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: string;
  CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET: string;
  WEBHOOK_URL: string;
  SYSTEM_PROMPT: string;
}

// Built on the first request rather than at import, because the API key and
// the bindings only exist inside a request.
let handler: ReturnType<typeof chatcopsCloudflareHandler> | undefined;
function getHandler(env: Env) {
  handler ??= chatcopsCloudflareHandler({
    provider: {
      type: 'claude',
      apiKey: env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY,
      model: 'claude-haiku-5-5',
    },

    systemPrompt: env.SYSTEM_PROMPT || `You are a helpful customer support assistant.
        Be friendly and concise. Capture leads when users share contact info.`,

    tools: [
      new LeadCaptureTool(async (lead) => {
        // Forward to webhook
        if (env.WEBHOOK_URL) {
          await fetch(env.WEBHOOK_URL, {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
            body: JSON.stringify({ type: 'lead', ...lead }),
          });
        }
      }),
    ],

    knowledge: [
      new FAQKnowledgeSource([
        { question: 'Pricing?', answer: 'Free tier available. Pro starts at $29/mo.' },
        { question: 'Support hours?', answer: '24/7 via chat, email support 9am-5pm EST.' },
        { question: 'Refund policy?', answer: '30-day money-back guarantee on all plans.' },
      ]),
      new TextKnowledgeSource([
        `We are a SaaS company specializing in developer tools.
          Founded in 2023. Based in San Francisco.
          Used by 1000+ developers worldwide.`,
      ]),
    ],

    storage: cloudflareStorage({ d1: env.DB, counters: env.COUNTERS }),
    retention: { conversationsDays: 30 },
    allowedOrigins: ['https://your-site.com'],
    sessionSecrets: [env.CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET],
    rateLimit: { maxRequests: 20, windowMs: 60_000 },
    budgets: { globalPerDay: { tokens: 20_000_000 } },
    analytics: true,
  });
  return handler;
}

export default {
  async fetch(request: Request, env: Env, ctx: ExecutionContext): Promise<Response> {
    // Health check
    if (new URL(request.url).pathname === '/health') {
      return new Response(JSON.stringify({ status: 'ok' }), {
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      });
    }

    // ctx lets lead webhooks and analytics writes finish after the reply.
    return getHandler(env)(request, env, ctx);
  },
};
```

Vercel Edge Full Example > Next.js Integration

If using Next.js, place the file at `app/api/chat/route.ts`:

```typescript
import { chatcopsVercelHandler } from '@chatcops/server';

export const runtime = 'edge';

const handler = chatcopsVercelHandler({
  provider: {
    type: 'openai',
    apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY!,
  },
  systemPrompt: 'You are a helpful assistant.',
  allowedOrigins: ['*'],
  sessionSecrets: [process.env.CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET!],
});

export const POST = handler;
// The handler answers the CORS preflight, so a widget on another origin needs OPTIONS too.
export const OPTIONS = handler;
```

Then `app/api/chat/session/route.ts`, for the session token: `export { POST, OPTIONS } from '../route';`.

More runtimes (Hono, Bun, Deno) are in [Next.js, Hono, Bun and Deno](/server/fetch-runtimes/).

Tools > Custom Tool Example

`inputSchema` takes any JSON Schema with an object at the root: nested objects, arrays, `enum`, `anyOf` and `$ref` work with Claude, OpenAI and Gemini.

```typescript
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';
import type { ChatTool } from '@chatcops/core';

const orderTool: ChatTool = {
  name: 'place_order',
  description: 'Place an order for one or more products',
  inputSchema: {
    type: 'object',
    properties: {
      email: { type: 'string', description: 'Customer email' },
      items: {
        type: 'array',
        minItems: 1,
        items: {
          type: 'object',
          properties: {
            sku: { type: 'string' },
            qty: { type: 'integer', minimum: 1 },
          },
          required: ['sku', 'qty'],
        },
      },
    },
    required: ['email', 'items'],
  },

  async execute(input, context) {
    const response = await fetch('https://shop.example.com/api/orders', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify(input),
      signal: context?.signal,
    });
    const order = await response.json();
    return { success: true, data: order, message: `Order ${order.id} placed` };
  },
};

// Use in server config
chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: '...' },
  systemPrompt: 'Help customers order products.',
  tools: [orderTool],
});
```

The model fills in the input, so check it in `execute` before acting on it, the way `LeadCaptureTool` does with zod.

Knowledge Base > KnowledgeSource Interface

```typescript
import type { RetrievalQuery, RetrievedChunk } from '@chatcops/core';

interface KnowledgeSource {
  type: string;
  getContext(query: string): Promise<string>;
  // Optional: the matching chunks themselves, with the conversation so far.
  retrieve?(query: RetrievalQuery): Promise<RetrievedChunk[]>;
}
```

When a source has `retrieve`, the server uses it instead of `getContext`. It passes the visitor's message and the last few messages before it, so a follow-up like "how long does it take?" can be searched in context. The chunks from every source are numbered and given to the model in one fenced block, marked as reference material that never contains instructions, since crawled pages sometimes do. That's what citations will build on.

Cloudflare Workers Adapter > Setup

Create a Worker at `src/index.ts`:

```typescript
import { chatcopsCloudflareHandler } from '@chatcops/server';

// Built on the first request rather than at import, because the API key is a
// Worker secret that only exists inside a request. Caching the handler here is
// what keeps conversation history and rate limiting alive across requests.
let handler: ReturnType<typeof chatcopsCloudflareHandler> | undefined;
function getHandler(env: Record<string, string>) {
  handler ??= chatcopsCloudflareHandler({
    provider: {
      type: 'claude',
      apiKey: env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY,
    },
    systemPrompt: 'You are a helpful assistant.',
    allowedOrigins: ['*'],
    // Signs the session tokens the widget gets from /session (a Worker sees every path).
    sessionSecrets: [env.CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET],
  });
  return handler;
}

export default {
  async fetch(
    request: Request,
    env: Record<string, string>
  ): Promise<Response> {
    return getHandler(env)(request);
  },
};
```

:::note[History and rate limits]
Conversation history and rate limiting live in memory on the isolate, not in a
shared store. They are not shared between isolates and they do not survive a
restart, so a deploy or a scale event resets both.
:::

Testing your bot > Unit tests with a scripted model > Testing a store

If you write your own storage, `runStorageConformance` runs the checks every built-in store passes. It covers saving and appending, version conflicts, counters, expiry, events and, when the store has `vectors`, vector search:

```typescript
import { runStorageConformance } from '@chatcops/core/testing';
import { memoryStorage } from '@chatcops/core';

runStorageConformance('my store', () => memoryStorage(), { ttlMs: 1500 });
```

Call it at the top level of a Vitest file. `ttlMs` (150 by default) is the expiry the checks use, and they wait about twice that, so raise it for a backend that rounds expiry to seconds. `skip` leaves out checks a backend can't meet in a test run: `'kv-ttl'`, `'counter-ttl'`, `'conversation-concurrency'`, `'counter-concurrency'` or `'vector-keyword-cjk'`. For example, Workers KV's shortest TTL is 60 seconds, so skip `'kv-ttl'` there.

Storage > Vector stores

`vectors` holds embedded chunks of your site's pages, for answering from them. The server doesn't search it yet; retrieval and citations build on it next. The interface is in place now so store packages can add it.

`memoryVectors` keeps vectors in the process and compares a query with every record. Use it for tests, local development, and indexes of a few thousand chunks:

```typescript
import { memoryStorage, memoryVectors } from '@chatcops/core';

const storage = {
  ...memoryStorage(),
  vectors: memoryVectors({ dimensions: 1024 }),
};

await storage.vectors.upsert([
  {
    id: 'pricing-0',
    vector: new Float32Array(1024),
    text: 'The Team plan is $20 a month.',
    source: { id: 'https://example.com/pricing', url: 'https://example.com/pricing', title: 'Pricing' },
    metadata: { locale: 'en' },
  },
]);

const results = await storage.vectors.query({
  vector: new Float32Array(1024),
  topK: 5,
  filter: { locale: ['en', 'de'] },
});
```

A `VectorStore` needs:

| Method | What it does |
|---|---|
| `dimensions` | The length of every vector. Storing or searching with another length is an error. |
| `upsert(records)` | Adds records, replacing any with the same id. |
| `query({ vector, topK, filter })` | The nearest records by cosine similarity, best first. |
| `keywordQuery({ text, topK, filter })` | Optional full-text search, for hybrid retrieval. Words are split with `Intl.Segmenter`, so Japanese and Chinese work. |
| `deleteBySource(sourceId)` | Removes every chunk of a page. |

A filter matches when every key matches: a value has to be equal and of the same type, and an array matches any of its values. Results don't include the vectors.

To check your own store, run the conformance suite from `@chatcops/core/testing`. Its vector checks run when the storage has `vectors`, need at least 4 dimensions, and tag every record with the run's id, so they work against a shared index.

