Quick Start > 2. Set Up the Server

Install the server package:

```bash
npm install @chatcops/server express
```

Make a secret for the session tokens the widget fetches before it chats, and keep it with your API key (never in client code):

```bash
openssl rand -base64 32   # put it in CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET
```

Create a minimal Express server:

```typescript
import express from 'express';
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

// app.use, so /chat/session, where the widget gets its token, reaches it too.
app.use('/chat', chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider: {
    type: 'claude',
    apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY!,
    model: 'claude-haiku-5-5',
  },
  systemPrompt: `You are a helpful customer support assistant for our website.
    Be friendly, concise, and helpful.`,
  allowedOrigins: ['*'],
  sessionSecrets: [process.env.CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET!],
  rateLimit: { maxRequests: 30, windowMs: 60_000 },
}));

app.listen(3001, () => {
  console.log('ChatCops server running on http://localhost:3001');
});
```

Without `sessionSecrets` the server refuses to start, so a chat can't be left open by accident. To try it without one, set `allowAnonymous: true` instead. See [Session tokens](/server/setup/#session-tokens).

AI Providers > Custom providers

Register a factory under a name of your own, then use that name as the provider `type`. The factory gets the whole provider config:

```typescript
import { registerProvider } from '@chatcops/core';
import type { AIProvider, ProviderChatParams, ProviderConfig } from '@chatcops/core';
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';

class MyProvider implements AIProvider {
  name = 'my-llm';
  constructor(_config: ProviderConfig) {}
  async *chat(_params: ProviderChatParams): AsyncGenerator<string> { yield 'Hello'; }
}

registerProvider('my-llm', async (config) => new MyProvider(config));

chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider: { type: 'my-llm', model: 'my-model' },
  systemPrompt: '...',
});
```

Registering a built-in name (`claude`, `openai`, `gemini`, `openai-compatible`) throws. Registering the same name again replaces the factory.

You can also skip the registry and pass a provider object you built yourself as the server's `provider`. Anything with `step()` or `chat()` works:

```typescript
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';
import type { AIProvider } from '@chatcops/core';

const provider: AIProvider = {
  name: 'my-llm',
  async *chat() { yield 'Hello'; },
};

chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider,
  systemPrompt: '...',
});
```

