Storage > Postgres

The Postgres store is provided by `@chatcops/store-postgres`. It is currently private and will ship with the next release, so install it alongside the server package when that release is available:

```bash
npm install @chatcops/server @chatcops/store-postgres pg
```

Create a `pg` client, run the store migration once, and pass the resulting store through the `storage` option:

```typescript
import { Pool } from 'pg';
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';
import { migrate, postgresStorage } from '@chatcops/store-postgres';

const db = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });

await migrate(db);

const storage = postgresStorage({ db, tablePrefix: 'chatcops_' });

const handler = chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider: {
    type: 'claude',
    apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY!,
  },
  systemPrompt: 'You are a helpful customer support assistant.',
  storage,
});
```

`tablePrefix` is optional and is useful when several applications share one database. Pass the same prefix to `migrate` and `postgresStorage`, and run migrations before the server handles traffic. Migrations are safe to run on every deploy because already-applied migrations are skipped:

```typescript
import { Pool } from 'pg';
import { neon } from '@neondatabase/serverless';
import { migrate } from '@chatcops/store-postgres';

const db = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });
await migrate(db, { tablePrefix: 'chatcops_' });
```

Upgrading to 1.0 > `@chatcops/server` > `cors` is now `allowedOrigins`

```typescript
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';

chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY! },
  systemPrompt: 'You are helpful.',
  // Before: cors: 'https://acme.com'
  allowedOrigins: ['https://acme.com'],
});
```

`allowedOrigins` takes several sites and subdomain wildcards like `'https://*.acme.com'`. `['*']` (the default) lets any site use the chat, as `cors: '*'` did. See [Allowed origins](/server/setup/#allowed-origins).

AI SDK (useChat) > Next.js

Create `app/api/chat/route.ts`, the path `useChat` calls by default:

```typescript
import { chatcopsAiSdkHandler } from '@chatcops/server';

const handler = chatcopsAiSdkHandler({
  provider: {
    type: 'claude',
    apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY!,
  },
  systemPrompt: 'You are a helpful assistant.',
  // useChat doesn't fetch session tokens.
  allowAnonymous: true,
});

export const POST = handler;
export const OPTIONS = handler;
```

`useChat` doesn't fetch [session tokens](/server/setup/#session-tokens), so this route sets `allowAnonymous: true`. Without it, the server refuses to start with no `sessionSecrets`, and refuses requests without a token when they're set. To use tokens anyway, set `sessionSecrets`, get a token from `/api/chat/session` for the chat id, and send it as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` with the headers of `useChat`'s transport.

Then use `useChat` as usual:

```tsx
'use client';
import { useChat } from '@ai-sdk/react';
import { useState } from 'react';

export function Chat({ chatId }: { chatId: string }) {
  const { messages, sendMessage, status, error } = useChat({ id: chatId });
  const [input, setInput] = useState('');

  return (
    <form
      onSubmit={(event) => {
        event.preventDefault();
        sendMessage({ text: input });
        setInput('');
      }}
    >
      {messages.map((message) => (
        <p key={message.id}>
          {message.role}:{' '}
          {message.parts.map((part, i) => (part.type === 'text' ? <span key={i}>{part.text}</span> : null))}
        </p>
      ))}
      {error && <p>Something went wrong. Try again.</p>}
      <input value={input} onChange={(event) => setInput(event.target.value)} disabled={status !== 'ready'} />
    </form>
  );
}
```

The chat `id` is the ChatCops conversation id. `useChat` makes up a new one each time the component mounts, so pass a stable `id` (one per conversation, saved however your app saves things) to keep talking in the same conversation after a reload.

API Reference > Methods > `ChatCops.setUser(user)`

Tell the widget who the signed-in visitor is.

- `token` is the identity token for [signed-in visitors](/server/setup/#signed-in-visitors), which your server signs.
- `name` and `email` are sent to the bot as visitor details with the first message of a conversation, and the name greets them.

Switching to another user, or `setUser(null)` when the visitor signs out, clears the chat, so one person's conversation never shows for the next. With the script tag build, a call before the chat has loaded is kept until it does.

```typescript
declare const session: { name: string; email: string; chatToken: string };

ChatCops.setUser({ name: session.name, email: session.email, token: session.chatToken });

// When the visitor signs out:
ChatCops.setUser(null);
```

Security > A production config > Cloudflare Workers

```typescript
import { chatcopsCloudflareHandler } from '@chatcops/server';
import { redisStorage } from '@chatcops/store-redis';
import { Redis } from '@upstash/redis/cloudflare';

let chat: ReturnType<typeof chatcopsCloudflareHandler> | undefined;

export default {
  fetch(request: Request, env: Env, ctx: ExecutionContext) {
    // Secrets only exist inside a request, so build the handler on the first one.
    chat ??= chatcopsCloudflareHandler({
      provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY },
      systemPrompt: 'You are the Acme support assistant.',
      storage: redisStorage({ redis: Redis.fromEnv(env) }),
      allowedOrigins: ['https://acme.com'],
      sessionSecrets: [env.CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET],
      turnstile: { secretKey: env.TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY },
      budgets: { globalPerDay: { tokens: 20_000_000 } },
    });
    return chat(request, env, ctx);
  },
};
```

The Worker gets every path, so `/session` needs nothing extra. The visitor's IP comes from `CF-Connecting-IP`.

