Widget Configuration > Programmatic Configuration > Saved conversations

The widget keeps the conversation so a visitor who reloads the page or comes back later picks up where they left off. `persistence` picks where:

| Value | Kept in | Lasts |
|-------|---------|-------|
| `'local'` (default) | `localStorage` | Until `historyTtlDays` passes, 30 days by default |
| `'session'` | `sessionStorage` | Until the tab is closed |
| `'none'` | Memory | Until the page reloads |

```typescript
ChatCops.init({
  apiUrl: '/api/chat',
  persistence: 'session',
});

// Or keep it in the browser, for a week
ChatCops.init({
  apiUrl: '/api/chat',
  historyTtlDays: 7,
});
```

A popup doesn't read or write storage until the visitor opens it. Until then, nothing is stored on the visitor's device. An inline chat is on screen from the start, so it reads its saved conversation when it mounts. The one exception is a welcome bubble with `showOnce`. It checks and sets its own flag when it's due to appear, and stores nothing before that. With `persistence: 'none'` nothing at all is written to storage, the bubble flag included.

The pre-chat form's answers are saved with the conversation, so they expire with it too.

`persistHistory` was removed in 1.0. A config that still sets it throws, so a site that asked for nothing to be saved never starts saving conversations. Use `persistence: 'none'` instead.

Upgrading to 1.0 > `@chatcops/server` > Session tokens are required by default

A chat request now needs a session token unless you say otherwise, so a leaked or guessed conversation id is useless. The widget fetches tokens by itself (from 0.6), so for most sites the change is a secret and, on some platforms, a route:

```typescript
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';

chatcopsMiddleware({
  provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY! },
  systemPrompt: 'You are helpful.',
  // openssl rand -base64 32, kept on the server
  sessionSecrets: [process.env.CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET!],
});
```

- A server without `sessionSecrets` refuses to start, with a message saying what to set. Add a secret, or set `allowAnonymous: true` to keep the 0.x behavior.
- `requireSession` is gone. `requireSession: false` (tokens checked when sent, not required) is now `allowAnonymous: true` next to `sessionSecrets`. `requireSession: true` was already the default with `sessionSecrets`, so delete it.
- The widget asks `<chat url>/session` for its token, so that path has to reach the handler. With Express, mount it with `app.use('/chat', handler)`, not `app.all('/chat', handler)`. On Vercel and Next.js, add a route file at `/api/chat/session` that re-exports the chat route. Workers, Hono with both paths, Bun and Deno need nothing more. See [Session tokens](/server/setup/#session-tokens).
- Widgets older than 0.6 and the AI SDK's `useChat` don't send tokens. For those, set `allowAnonymous: true`.
- `chatcops test` runs your server in process and doesn't need tokens, whatever the config says.

Storage > Vector stores > Upstash Vector

The Upstash store uses an existing dense index configured for cosine distance. Set its dimensions to match your embedder. The store preserves the page source in reserved metadata fields, so `deleteBySource()` can remove all of a page's chunks. Configure a dedicated test index with at least four dimensions to run conformance; tests do not reset the index.

```bash
npm install @chatcops/store-upstash-vector @upstash/vector
```

```typescript
import { memoryStorage } from '@chatcops/core';
import { Index } from '@upstash/vector';
import { upstashVectors } from '@chatcops/store-upstash-vector';

const dimensions = 1024;
const index = new Index({
  url: process.env.UPSTASH_VECTOR_REST_URL!,
  token: process.env.UPSTASH_VECTOR_REST_TOKEN!,
});
const storage = {
  ...memoryStorage(),
  vectors: upstashVectors({ index, dimensions }),
};
```

Metadata filters require every key to match and accept any value in an array. For sparse indexes and hybrid indexes with a sparse text embedding model, pass `keywordSearch: true` to expose `keywordQuery`. Dense indexes leave that method absent. The SDK does not encode the configured index kind in its `Index` type, so only enable this option for supported indexes.

