Storage > Cloudflare D1

`@chatcops/store-cloudflare` stores conversations, turn locks and analytics events in D1. Rate limit and budget counters go to a Durable Object when you bind one, and to D1 otherwise. It also ships with the next release.

```typescript
// docs-check: skip, needs the Workers types and bindings from wrangler
import { chatcopsCloudflareHandler } from '@chatcops/server/cloudflare';
import { ChatCopsCounter, cloudflareStorage } from '@chatcops/store-cloudflare';

// Cloudflare finds the Durable Object class through the Worker's exports.
export { ChatCopsCounter };

export default {
  async fetch(request: Request, env: Env, ctx: ExecutionContext) {
    const handler = chatcopsCloudflareHandler({
      provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY },
      systemPrompt: 'You are a helpful customer support assistant.',
      storage: cloudflareStorage({ d1: env.DB, counters: env.COUNTERS }),
    });
    return handler(request, env, ctx);
  },
};
```

In `wrangler.jsonc`, bind the D1 database as `DB` and the counter class as `COUNTERS`, with a migration that lists `ChatCopsCounter` under `new_sqlite_classes`. D1 has no interactive transactions, so a write to a conversation is one `batch()` that checks the version first. Two isolates answering the same conversation can't both write, and the loser gets a `ConflictError` with nothing half written. Workers KV isn't used for locks, since it has no atomic set-if-absent.

Server Setup > Allowed origins

`allowedOrigins` lists the sites whose pages can use the chat. Each entry is an exact origin, or a wildcard for subdomains:

```typescript
import type { ChatCopsServerConfig } from '@chatcops/server';

const config: Partial<ChatCopsServerConfig> = {
  allowedOrigins: ['https://acme.com', 'https://*.acme.com'],
};
```

A browser request from any other page gets `403 {"error":"origin_not_allowed"}` before the model is called, and the server logs the origin it refused. Without the list (or with `['*']`), any site can put your chat on its pages and spend your tokens. Set it in production.

Entries need the scheme and no path: `https://acme.com`, not `acme.com` or `https://acme.com/`. `https://*.acme.com` matches `https://docs.acme.com` but not `https://acme.com` itself, so list both if you need both. Include the port when it isn't the default, as in `http://localhost:4321`.

Requests without an `Origin` header, such as `curl` or a call from your own server, still go through. The list stops other sites embedding your bot. It doesn't stop scripts, which can send any `Origin` they like. Rate limits and budgets are what stop those.

`cors` is the older option for one origin. It still works as a one-item `allowedOrigins`, with a deprecation warning.

Storage > Vector stores > SQLite with sqlite-vec

Install the optional peer dependency to keep embeddings in the same SQLite file:

```bash
npm install @chatcops/store-sqlite sqlite-vec
```

```typescript
import { sqliteStorage } from '@chatcops/store-sqlite';

const storage = sqliteStorage({
  path: './chatcops.db',
  vectors: { dimensions: 1024 },
});
```

Use the embedder's dimensions. `storage.vectors` supports cosine similarity, typed metadata filters, source deletion, and FTS5 keyword search with Japanese and Chinese word segmentation. Without `vectors`, SQLite storage does not load or require `sqlite-vec`. A caller-supplied database must be opened with `allowExtension: true` to enable vectors.

Tools > ChatTool Interface

Create custom tools by implementing the `ChatTool` interface:

```typescript
import type { JSONSchema, MessagePart, ToolParameter } from '@chatcops/core';

interface ChatTool {
  name: string;          // letters, digits, _ and -, starting with a letter or _ (64 at most)
  description: string;
  inputSchema?: JSONSchema;                     // preferred: JSON Schema, root type 'object'
  parameters?: Record<string, ToolParameter>;   // the older flat form
  required?: string[];                          // with parameters only
  annotations?: { title?: string; readOnlyHint?: boolean; destructiveHint?: boolean };
  execute(input: Record<string, unknown>, context?: ToolContext): Promise<ToolResult>;
  // Optional: parts to add to the reply after the tool runs.
  // LeadCaptureTool adds a `lead-captured` part, which the widget reports.
  emits?(result: ToolResult): MessagePart[];
}

interface ToolContext {
  conversationId: string;
  signal: AbortSignal;   // aborts when the visitor leaves or the tool times out; pass it to fetch()
}

interface ToolResult {
  success: boolean;
  data?: unknown;
  message?: string;
}
```

The server checks every tool when it starts and throws a `ToolDefinitionError` listing what's wrong (an invalid or duplicate name, a schema whose root isn't an object, both `inputSchema` and `parameters` set), so a mistake shows up at boot instead of on the first message.

API Reference > Methods > `ChatCops.init(config)`

Initialize the widget with a configuration object. Must be called before any other method if not using script tag auto-init.

```typescript
// Popup mode (default)
ChatCops.init({
  apiUrl: 'https://your-api.com/chat',
  theme: { accent: '#6366f1' },
  branding: { name: 'My Bot' },
  welcomeMessage: 'Hello! How can I help?',
});

// Inline mode
ChatCops.init({
  apiUrl: 'https://your-api.com/chat',
  mode: 'inline',
  container: '#chat-container',
  branding: { name: 'My Bot' },
});
```

Calling `init()` again will destroy the previous instance and create a new one.

In the ESM and single-file builds, `init()` returns the `Widget` instance. In the script tag loader (`chatcops.min.js`) it returns nothing, since the chat itself loads later. Use the `ChatCops` methods there, which work before and after the chat has loaded.

